#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77cfab07-1ced-53a5-9c30-1fdc71ce1fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271557} {:answer/id #uuid "596201c2-5166-51c3-b347-e55714af0c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271558} {:answer/id #uuid "858df574-af1c-5332-ad4c-76bba9f41ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271559} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb6371d-dc04-577b-ac3c-59dd08d86b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271560} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6a33ce-6a60-5b2d-9310-cd5ffd8b2945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "596201c2-5166-51c3-b347-e55714af0c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271558} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb77417a-e174-52a2-acff-95872f70db10" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b93b20-63af-5abb-b512-c02cecaedf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-2545" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f59f65e184ccf4fdf2ae2124543ece45d41afce09d743dec9f17730dd00124d4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circle above is increasing. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference." :question/worked-solution "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{'}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f825c9f6-fa66-5744-9891-f93ad569990e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(1\\,699)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272350} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4ae87d-ca20-5ec3-ab30-b42ad74b467a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(1\\,699)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272351} {:answer/id #uuid "0a9884b4-5900-5d9b-a23f-dcb291c1bb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1\\,699)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272352} {:answer/id #uuid "42090516-9715-5392-8c32-b922ea5ea87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,699" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272353} {:answer/id #uuid "680464eb-4cd1-5cb5-93cc-7c8e95b9261f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(1\\,699)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42090516-9715-5392-8c32-b922ea5ea87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,699" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272353} :answer-field/id #uuid "82a88222-952e-5819-9181-f73b5a076050" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4df84652-8096-5b0a-ac62-74634ce27764" :question/math-academy-id "q-48700" :question/problem "The number of computers affected by the spread of some ransomware after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $f(t)=\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change in the number of infected computers when $t=9$ days?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{'}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{100(3{t}^{2})(t+1)-100({t}^{3}+2)(1)}{{(t+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-2}{{(t+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(9) & =100\\cdot \\frac{2(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-2}{{((9)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1\\,458+243-2}{100} \\\\  & =1\\,699.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ is $1\\,699$ computers per day."}]}
